# C# style

Apply when creating a script or editing an existing one. Do not rewrite untouched files.

## Line length

Keep a statement, call, or signature on one line if it is about 100 characters or less. Collection initializers stay on that line when they fit: `new[] { queen.Id, knight.Id }`. Wrap only past that threshold. When wrapping, keep `(` on the same line as the name.

## Spacing

No blank line between members in the same region. One blank line between regions. No blank line after `#region` or before `#endregion`.
No blank lines inside a method.
If the body is a single `return`, keep it on one line: `if (_material == null) return;`

## Regions

Omit a region if it would be empty. Order:

1. `Fields` — serialized refs, then private state, then properties
2. `Unity` — Awake, Start, Update, OnDestroy, and other messages
3. `Public Methods`
4. `Private Methods`
5. `Debug` or another context-specific region only when that file has one

## Comments

No `//`, `/* */`, or commented-out code. Names carry meaning. `#region` / `#endregion` are the only labels. `///` only on public methods that are a stable, often-called API (Core types other assemblies use). Not on Unity messages, private helpers, or one-off UI/Match methods.

## Prefabs

Prefer a prefab over `new GameObject` + `AddComponent` trees. Instantiate and tune the prefab. Plain Core C# stays prefab-free. Do not mass-convert existing bootstrap HUD; only new work and files we edit.

## References

On MonoBehaviours, `[SerializeField]` the reference and assign it on the prefab or scene. No `Find`, `FindObjectOfType`, or hierarchy search for something that can be dragged in. `GetComponent` on `this` or a known child is fine. `RuntimePrefabs.Load` is an editor fallback when a serialized ref is missing, not the normal path. Do not serialize scratch values, counters, or flags.
